  "query_preview": "..\n\nLargely uneventful long term in terms of impact, but several\ninteresting features and tidbits. The first being Tuesday morning,\nas the stronger low moves down from the NE gulf coast towards\nDixon Entrance. Rather strong surge of cold air looks to be\nassociated with this low; and with some leeside troughing from \nnorthwesterly winds, it could be as low as 990 mb near Yakutat\nwith 850 mb temperatures dropping below 0 C. As a result,\nexpecting lapse rates around the low center to be stronger. With\nstrong vorticity advection, expecting plenty of lift with the\npossibility of thunderstorms in the outer coast. Most of the rain\naccumulations look to be on the outer coast with persistent\nonshore flow, but since this low looks relatively transient, not\nexpecting any one area to have extended periods of moderate to\npossibly heavy rainfall.\n\nAs it moves towards the southern panhandle, another shortwave\ntrough looks to come across the gulf waters, merging with the\nexisting low near the panhandle, strengthening it again. There is\nsome uncertainty on exactly how this shortwave will merge with the\nongoing system, but definitely trending towards impacting the\nCanadian areas. \n\nGoing into the mid to late week, high amplitude ridging returns\nwith northwesterly flow, drier conditions, and warmer\ntemperatures.",
